Dear Roger,

Julie and I read the box 12 receipts against the working Table 2.1 yesterday.=
 I have attached the corrected table and note (LHT-20260112-EVD-001, v0.3). T=
he old heading, =E2=80=9CParticipants=E2=80=9D, suggested a count of people; =
I have changed it to =E2=80=9CPayment entries=E2=80=9D.

The four receipt-date quarters still give 42, 49, 45 and 48, a total of 184 e=
ntries. Bernard's instalment and later balance are two receipts on different =
dates; Roux's single handover for three subscriptions is one entry. The table=
 therefore counts recorded receipts, not individuals. In particular, the Janu=
ary 1864 receipt of a December balance belongs in the January=E2=80=93March r=
eceipt quarter.

May I use the revised heading and note in our internal working chapter, and a=
dd this sentence to the discussion: =E2=80=9CThe number of payment entries is=
 not a denominator for an attendance rate, since the accounts do not establis=
h how many people came to the room.=E2=80=9D I would like the next draft to e=
xplain to the reader what the four periods count and why the total cannot bea=
r a claim about visits.

This is for the working draft; I am not asking to replace the chapter version=
 sent to Edward in February.

Best,
Judith


